  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 330 PM CDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nLow pressure was located over northern Manitoba early this\nafternoon with a cold front extending into northwestern \nOntario, central Minnesota and western Iowa. A line of showers \nwas observed on radar ahead of this cold front from northeastern\nMinnesota into central Minnesota. A large area of high pressure\nwas centered over the northeastern CONUS with another area in \nthe northern High Plains. As the cold front moves to the east \ninto tonight, showers will end from west to east. An isolated \nlightning strike or two cannot be ruled out with this activity, \nbut instability is very limited and lightning activity has \nceased after being present earlier this morning. Breezy \nsoutherly slow ahead of the cold front has resulted in \ntemperatures warming into the 60s and 70s from the Minnesota \nArrowhead into the I-35 corridor and across northwest Wisconsin.\n\nA much more seasonable airmass arrives tonight behind the cold \nfront as high pressure builds in from the west. Highs tomorrow\nwill only be in the upper 40s and 50s with mainly dry\nconditions. The cold front may linger across northern Wisconsin\ninto southern Minnesota and bring a stray shower or two to our\nsouthern row of counties in northwest Wisconsin. With the drier\nair moving in and northwest winds gusting to 15 to 20 mph across\nnortheastern Minnesota, RH values may fall into the 25 to 35%\nrange during the afternoon. Much of northern Minnesota away from\nLake Superior will fall below freezing tomorrow night with\nmid-20s possible in north-central Minnesota. \n\nHigh pressure will be to our north on Monday before shifting to\nthe east Monday night into Tuesday. Surface low pressure will\nmove from the Central Plains to the Red River Valley of the\nNorth Monday night into Tuesday and bring chances for showers to\nthe region starting Monday night. Another upper low will emerge\nfrom the Rockies Tuesday night into Wednesday with another area\nof surface low pressure developing across the Northern Plains. \nThis will keep chances for showers in place across the Northland\ninto Wednesday afternoon. Additional rainfall chances will then\nbe seen for the end of the week. Highs Monday and Tuesday will\nbe in the 40s and 50s before warming into the 50s and 60s for\nthe remainder of the week. With lows Monday night into Tuesday\nmorning falling below freezing across northern Minnesota, so\nsnow may mix with the rain or possibly change over to all snow\nfor a brief period before temperatures trend warmer after\nsunrise Tuesday morning. No accumulations are expected, but\ndon't be surprised to see some white stuff in the air in spots\nearly Tuesday morning.",
